### Should you prefer composition or inheritance?

The Gang of Four says favor composition over inheritance. Here is why.

### What is the fragile base class problem?

```java
public class Account {
    protected java.math.BigDecimal balance = java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO;
    public void deposit(java.math.BigDecimal amount) { balance = balance.add(amount); }
    public void depositAll(java.util.List<java.math.BigDecimal> amounts) {
        for (java.math.BigDecimal amount : amounts) { deposit(amount); }
    }
}

public class AuditedAccount extends Account {
    private int transactionCount = 0;
    @Override
    public void deposit(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        super.deposit(amount);
        transactionCount++;
    }
}
// depositAll([100, 200, 300]) -> transactionCount = 3 (correct now)
// But if Account changes depositAll() to NOT call deposit(), count breaks.
// The child's behavior depends on the parent's internal implementation: fragile.
```

### How does composition fix this?

```java
public class AuditedAccount {
    private final Account account; // HAS-A (delegation)
    private int transactionCount = 0;

    public AuditedAccount(Account account) { this.account = account; }

    public void deposit(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        account.deposit(amount); // delegates
        transactionCount++;
    }

    public void depositAll(java.util.List<java.math.BigDecimal> amounts) {
        for (java.math.BigDecimal amount : amounts) { this.deposit(amount); }
        // We control the flow. Parent changes do not break us.
    }
}
```

### What are the four reasons to prefer composition?

```java
// Reason 1: Flexibility (swap at runtime)
public class TradeService {
    private final FeeCalculator feeCalculator; // injected
    public TradeService(FeeCalculator feeCalculator) { this.feeCalculator = feeCalculator; }
}
// Production: new TradeService(new StandardFeeCalculator())
// Testing: new TradeService(new ZeroFeeCalculator())
// Promotion: new TradeService(new DiscountedFeeCalculator())

// Reason 2: Multiple capabilities
public class TradeService {
    private final FeeCalculator feeCalc;         // has-a
    private final NotificationService notifier;  // has-a
    private final AuditService auditor;          // has-a
}
// Inheritance: only extends one class.

// Reason 3: No fragile base class problem
// Composition delegates. Does not inherit internals.

// Reason 4: Controlled exposure
// Inheritance exposes ALL parent public methods.
// Composition exposes only what it chooses to delegate.
```

### When should you still use inheritance?

- True IS-A relationship: SavingsAccount IS-A Account (business domain truth).
- Shared mutable state: balance field shared across account types.
- Template Method Pattern: parent defines algorithm, child fills steps.
- Framework requirement: extends HttpServlet.

### How do they compare?

| Aspect | Inheritance | Composition |
|--------|------------|-------------|
| Coupling | Tight | Loose |
| Flexibility | Fixed at compile time | Swappable at runtime |
| Reuse count | Single extends | Multiple has-a |
| Exposure | All parent methods | Only delegated methods |
| Fragile base class | Yes | No |
| Use case | True IS-A with state | Everything else |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- In Spring, composition is the default: services have repositories, have other services, all injected via constructor.
- Effective Java Item 18: favor composition over inheritance.
- The Decorator pattern is a specific form of composition where the wrapper implements the same interface as the delegate.
